WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.670
<v Pledgivo>Post-install setup walkthrough

2
00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:25.920
<v Start here>The Get Started panel — Open App Launcher > Pledgivo > Settings

3
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:39.370
<v 1. Permission sets>The four sets the package installs — Look, don't change - none of them is assigned yet

4
00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:45.350
<v 1. Permission sets>Fundraising Admin — Confirm it carries the package namespace

5
00:00:45.350 --> 00:01:00.050
<v 1. Permission sets>Manage Assignments — This lists who already holds the set - it is not how you add yourself

6
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:07.250
<v 1. Permission sets>The route that always works — Open your user, scroll to Permission Set Assignments

7
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.930
<v 1. Permission sets>The assignment picker — Click Edit Assignments

8
00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:18.660
<v 1. Permission sets>Choose the set — Select Fundraising Admin under Available Permission Sets

9
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.040
<v 1. Permission sets>Move it across — Click Add, then Save

10
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:29.040
<v 1. Permission sets>Assigned — Confirm Fundraising Admin now appears on the user

11
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:39.710
<v 1. Permission sets>Step 1 closes itself — Reload the console - the panel re-queries the org, you never tick a box

12
00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:48.780
<v 2. Record types>Record type mapping — Nothing to do - the install seeds all seven records

13
00:01:48.780 --> 00:02:00.700
<v 3. Experience site>The Site & Domain checklist — Read which steps the app can verify and which are yours to confirm

14
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:10.320
<v 3. Experience site>No site yet — Click New

15
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:18.540
<v 3. Experience site>The template gallery — Ignore the Aura templates - the package does not target them

16
00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.920
<v 3. Experience site>Pick the right template — Choose Build Your Own (LWR), then Get Started

17
00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:33.970
<v 3. Experience site>Name it — Enter a name and a short, permanent URL path - it lands in every donation link

18
00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:43.040
<v 3. Experience site>Experience Workspaces — Note both tiles: Builder designs pages, Administration activates the site

19
00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:51.090
<v 3. Experience site>A new site starts at Preview — Read the status - Preview means admins only, not the public

20
00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:57.240
<v 3. Experience site>Builder settings — Open the gear icon > Settings > General

21
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:05.770
<v 3. Experience site>Let guests in — Tick 'guest users can see and interact with the site without logging in'

22
00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:09.870
<v 3. Experience site>Publish — Click Publish, then confirm

23
00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:19.220
<v 3. Experience site>Publishing is asynchronous — Wait for the confirmation email before changing anything else

24
00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:28.620
<v 4. Connect Stripe>The Payments guide — Keep this panel open in a second tab while you work in Setup

25
00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:37.370
<v 4. Connect Stripe>The values to paste — Copy each value from here - never type a secret into this app

26
00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:47.790
<v 4. Connect Stripe>External Credentials — The external credential holds the secret; the named credential holds the URL

27
00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:54.290
<v 4. Connect Stripe>New external credential — Click New on the External Credentials tab

28
00:03:54.290 --> 00:04:00.990
<v 4. Connect Stripe>The public credential — Label 'Stripe Public', name 'Stripe_Auth', protocol Custom

29
00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:12.560
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Saved — The Principals and Custom Headers lists appear only after this first save

30
00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:26.110
<v 4. Connect Stripe>The principal — Named Principal 'OrgPrincipal', parameter SecretKey = Bearer sk_...

31
00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:35.860
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Write-only from here on — The key is now unreadable - to change it you overwrite it

32
00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:40.690
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Custom header — Click New under Custom Headers

33
00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:50.490
<v 4. Connect Stripe>The merge field — Name Authorization, value {!$Credential.Stripe_Auth.SecretKey}

34
00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:57.820
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Public credential complete — One principal, one header - check both before moving on

35
00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:05.650
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Named Credentials — The external credential is not usable on its own

36
00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:11.680
<v 4. Connect Stripe>New named credential — Click New on the Named Credentials tab

37
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:22.980
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Point it at Stripe — Name Stripe_Public, URL https://api.stripe.com, EC = Stripe Public

38
00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:33.830
<v 4. Connect Stripe>The two callout options that matter — Allow Formulas in HTTP Header ON, Generate Authorization Header OFF

39
00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:39.630
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Now the admin pair — Repeat with name Stripe_Admin_Auth

40
00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:46.480
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Its own principal — Same shape, different key - the two credentials share no state

41
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:54.180
<v 4. Connect Stripe>The admin named credential — Name Stripe_Admin, same URL, same callout options

42
00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:05.980
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Both credentials exist — Public charges cards; admin issues refunds

43
00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:12.130
<v 4. Connect Stripe>Re-check — Back in the console, click Re-check to grade the finished build

44
00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:20.080
<v 4. Payment account>Payment Accounts — Click Add - a credential alone cannot take a donation

45
00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:26.080
<v 4. Payment account>Wire it up — Point it at BOTH named credentials and tick Active

46
00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.030
<v 4. Payment account>Saved and active — The org can now reach Stripe

47
00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:40.930
<v 5. Grant the credential>A permission set you build yourself — Click New - a managed permission set cannot carry this grant

48
00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:49.530
<v 5. Grant the credential>Name it for payments — Keep public and admin in separate sets - one is going to a guest user

49
00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:54.030
<v 5. Grant the credential>Created, empty — No grants on it yet

50
00:06:54.030 --> 00:07:07.830
<v 5. Grant the credential>The page nobody finds — Apps > External Credential Principal Access > Edit

51
00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:17.280
<v 5. Grant the credential>Enable the principal — Enable the PUBLIC credential's OrgPrincipal - check the credential name

52
00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.460
<v 5. Grant the credential>Saved — Click Save

53
00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:27.460
<v 5. Grant the credential>The admin set — Repeat on the second set for the ADMIN principal - staff only

54
00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:35.780
<v 5. Grant the credential>The site's guest user — Find it via Builder > Settings > General > guest user profile

55
00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:45.900
<v 5. Grant the credential>Assign the public set — Assign the PUBLIC payments set to the guest user - never the admin one

56
00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:51.780
<v 5. Grant the credential>Guest user ready — From this moment a public donation can reach Stripe

57
00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:59.330
<v 5. Grant the credential>Staff get the admin set — Assign it only to the people who take payments and issue refunds

58
00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:09.130
<v 5. Allowed namespaces>The field that blocks packaged callouts — Edit the named credential, enter the package namespace

59
00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:17.410
<v 5. Allowed namespaces>Managed Package Access — Confirm the namespace appears here, then repeat on the other credential

60
00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:26.530
<v 5. Allowed namespaces>Prove it end to end — Click Prove it works - a real callout, answered by your Stripe account

61
00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:34.030
<v 7. Name and logo>Five down — The panel names what is left rather than making you compare lists

62
00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:43.030
<v 7. Name and logo>Organization & Donations — Identity fields on the left, gift rules below - no mailing address here

63
00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:50.760
<v 7. Name and logo>Your name on the receipt — Set the display name and Tax ID, then Save

64
00:08:50.760 --> 00:09:01.180
<v Activate the site>Still Preview — Read the status column - the site has a URL but serves nothing publicly

65
00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:11.150
<v Activate the site>Everything here is already correct — Public Access on, Published - and there is no Activate button on this screen

66
00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:15.600
<v Activate the site>Publish the page changes — Click Publish

67
00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:24.320
<v Activate the site>Published, still not live — Publishing succeeded and the status is unchanged - this is the trap

68
00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:31.970
<v Activate the site>Preview - Published, side by side — Open Workspaces, then the Administration tile

69
00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:40.420
<v Activate the site>The Activate button — Click Activate - here and nowhere else. Accept the members-email warning

70
00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:46.220
<v Activate the site>Live — The public URL now serves the site to anyone

71
00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:56.570
<v Done>7 of 7 — Your org is ready to take donations - steps stay live and reopen if something breaks

72
00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:02.970
<v Wrap up>Your org is ready to take donations.

73
00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:32.650
<v About Pledgivo>Fundraising and donation management, native to Salesforce. Docs: cloudalgo.com/pledgivo-docs

